  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{6}={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $4x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}+{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
